  "question": {
    "text": "Considering the basic strength of amines in an aqueous solution, which one has the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub></math> value?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mi>NH</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Since&nbsp;in aromatic amines the lone pair undergoes delocalisation, aliphatic amines are more basic than aromatic amines.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ph</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br />In an aliphatic amine, due to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> effect of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;the order will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.<br />In an aqueous solution,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo></math>bonding takes place. So, the order will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.<br /><br />However, the resultant basic order is due to a mixture of both the effects(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;and hydrogen bond).&nbsp;So, the&nbsp;order is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.&nbsp;<br /><br />So, the final basic order is&nbsp;<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0362_b7311dd7b63c.jpg\" style=\"width: 339px; height: 99px;\" /></p>"
  }
}